        "excerpt": "Find out when pregnancy nausea usually begins and improves, which self-care measures may help and how to recognise severe vomiting or dehydration.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "It can begin around the time a period is due, but symptoms alone cannot confirm pregnancy. Use a pregnancy test at the recommended time and repeat or seek advice if the result is unclear.",
            "question": "Can morning sickness start before a positive test?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Yes. The name is misleading: pregnancy nausea can happen at any time and may last all day. Ask for medical help if you cannot eat or drink enough or daily life becomes difficult.",
            "question": "Is it normal to have morning sickness all day?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "No. Symptoms vary widely, and some healthy pregnancies involve little or no nausea. Contact your clinician for concerning symptoms such as pain or bleeding, rather than using nausea as a measure of pregnancy health.",
            "question": "Does no morning sickness mean something is wrong?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Pregnancy-safe anti-sickness medicines are available, but the right option depends on your health and symptoms. Ask your doctor, midwife or pharmacist rather than self-treating with herbal or over-the-counter products.",
            "question": "What can I take for morning sickness?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": false,
