        "excerpt": "How movement tracking works, why recommendations differ and what to do immediately if your baby’s usual pattern changes.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "If formal counting is recommended, it commonly begins around 28 weeks, but follow your own maternity team. Before then, learn your baby’s developing pattern.",
            "question": "When should I start kick counts?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "There is no universal normal number. Some local protocols use ten movements within a set time; the baby’s usual pattern and your clinician’s instructions matter most.",
            "question": "How many kicks should I feel?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Most formal methods count kicks, rolls, swishes and stretches but not rhythmic hiccups. Follow the method provided by your care team.",
            "question": "Does a hiccup count as movement?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": false,
