        "excerpt": "How much activity is recommended in an uncomplicated pregnancy, safe exercise ideas, useful modifications and warning signs to stop.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "Usually yes in an uncomplicated pregnancy. Start slowly and discuss your plan at an early prenatal visit, especially if you were inactive or have medical concerns.",
            "question": "Can I start exercising while pregnant?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Experienced runners may often continue with modifications and clinician approval. Stop for warning symptoms and adapt for balance, heat and comfort.",
            "question": "Can I run during pregnancy?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Many core exercises can be modified, but prolonged flat-on-the-back positions later in pregnancy and movements causing pain or abdominal doming need adjustment.",
            "question": "Are abdominal exercises safe?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": false,
        "heading": null,
        "heroImage": {
          "src": "https://cdn.sanity.io/images/hphu8zlu/production/4a20dfa9f5a0970d4b3bc8b585814ef97aaf5fc0-1672x941.png?w=1600&fit=max&auto=format",
          "alt": "Pregnant woman doing a gentle supported exercise in a light studio",
          "width": 1672,
          "height": 941,
          "credit": "HelloMommy editorial image generated with OpenAI"
        },
        "intro": "For most healthy people with an uncomplicated pregnancy, regular exercise is safe and beneficial. A common goal is at least 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity each week, adapted to fitness and medical advice. Choose low-risk activities, stay cool and hydrated, and avoid sports with a high risk of falling, collision or abdominal injury.",
        "locale": "en",
        "publishedAt": "2026-08-27",
