        "excerpt": "A practical guide to contractions, a show, waters breaking and the symptoms that mean it is time to call your maternity unit or get urgent help.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "Common early signs are period-like cramps, backache, pelvic pressure, a show and contractions that gradually become regular and stronger. Waters may break before or during labour.",
            "question": "What are the very first signs of labour?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Many maternity units ask you to call when contractions are regular and around five minutes apart, but your personal advice may differ. Call your unit before travelling and sooner if you have any warning sign.",
            "question": "How far apart should contractions be before hospital?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Yes. You may not notice a show, and it can happen days before labour. Regular, intensifying contractions or waters breaking are more useful signs to act on.",
            "question": "Can labour start without losing the mucus plug?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Call your maternity unit immediately for advice. They will ask about the fluid, your baby’s movements and your pregnancy, then explain when assessment or induction may be needed.",
            "question": "What if my waters break but contractions do not start?"
          }
        ],
