        "excerpt": "A trimester-by-trimester guide to common physical and emotional changes, practical ways to cope and warning symptoms that should be checked urgently.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "answer": "There is no universal answer. The first trimester often has the strongest hormonal symptoms, while the third can bring more physical pressure and sleep problems. Severity varies from person to person.",
            "question": "Which trimester has the most symptoms?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Nausea and fatigue often improve between about 12 and 20 weeks, but they can end earlier, later or continue. A sudden change alone is not a diagnosis; contact your clinician if you also have pain, bleeding or feel unwell.",
            "question": "When do first trimester symptoms ease?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Most people first notice movement between 16 and 24 weeks. Tell your midwife if you have not felt movement by 24 weeks, and call immediately if established movements reduce, change or stop.",
            "question": "When will I feel the baby move?"
          },
          {
            "answer": "Mild, brief cramps can occur as the uterus grows or because of constipation. Severe, persistent or one-sided pain, or pain with bleeding, fever, dizziness or shoulder pain, needs urgent medical advice.",
            "question": "Are cramps normal during pregnancy?"
          }
        ],
        "featured": false,
        "heading": null,
        "heroImage": {
          "src": "https://cdn.sanity.io/images/hphu8zlu/production/c8134a212ba033cafc822354b5551b25a386b252-1672x941.png?w=1600&fit=max&auto=format",
          "alt": "Pregnant woman showing the stages of a growing bump across three trimesters",
          "width": 1672,
          "height": 941,
          "credit": "HelloMommy editorial image generated with OpenAI"
        },
        "intro": "Pregnancy symptoms change as hormones shift, the uterus grows and your baby develops. The first trimester often brings fatigue, nausea and breast changes; the second may bring more energy, a growing bump and first movements; the third commonly adds pelvic pressure, breathlessness and sleep difficulty. Experiences vary, and severe or sudden symptoms deserve medical advice.",
        "locale": "en",
